The problem was not strictly related to the abort controller but interrupting the async generator.

While writing this comment I also found another issue which was related to consuming the stream with await for and doing an early break.

I updated and added a new test file with the reproduction which do not pass without the fix and use the full streamText function to make sure they reproduce the real problem.

Basically the smallest repro is this code:

```ts
const result = streamText({
  model,
  prompt: 'Generate text',
});

const uiStream = result.toUIMessageStream({
  onFinish: onFinishCallback,
  generateMessageId: () => 'msg-async-iter',
});

// Consume only first few chunks using async iteration, then break
let chunkCount = 0;

for await (const chunk of uiStream) {
  chunkCount++;

  if (chunkCount >= 5) {
    break; // This was not triggering the stream flush(). Instead it just stops consuming the stream and onfinish is never called
  }
}
// onFinish is never called
```

This code will cause the generator to stop and the stream to stop being consumed, the problem was in `createAsyncIterableStream` which does not pass a `return` method in the async iterator. return is necessary to cleanup resources when a `await for` loop breaks

I updated the code to cancel the stream when the async generator breaks. With the added cancel method in the `TransformStream` `onFinish` will be called.

Adding cancel in `TransofrmStream` now also handles the case when the user cancels the `UIMessageStreamResponse`, which is another problem that some users could face, some frameworks probably cancel responses when the request abort controller aborts to prevent memory leaks. Smallest repro is this code:

```tsx
const res = result.toUIMessageStreamResponse()
res.body?.cancel()
```

