It's possible to do it this way. I attached the simplest version here: [stop-when-example.ts](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/24083030/stop-when-example.ts). 

The problems with just `stopWhen`:
- requires at least one tool call
- requires state outside of `generateText` call
- recovery/repair instructions aren't explicit to the model output, instead being a tool

Below is minimal illustration of how to do with `stopWhen`:

```ts
let reviews: any[] = [];

await generateText({
  model: llm,
  system: systemPrompt,
  prompt: instructionPrompt,
  stopWhen: (step) => {
    // Stop if we have a passed review
    const passedReview = reviews.find(review => review.pass);
    if (passedReview) {
      return true;
    }
    // Otherwise, stop at step 5 (fallback limit)
    return stepCountIs(5)(step);
  },
  tools: tools,
  toolChoice: "required",
  onStepFinish: async ({ toolResults }) => {
    if (toolResults && toolResults.length > 0) {
      const reviewMessageResult = toolResults.find(result => result.toolName === 'reviewMessage');
      if (reviewMessageResult) {
        reviews.push(reviewMessageResult.output);
      }
    }
  },
});

const reviewedMessage = reviews.find(review => review.pass)?.passMessage;
console.log("reviewedMessage", reviewedMessage);
```

