@lgrammel I preferred explicit statements to focus on the logic of the solution. 
Instead of ‘freezing’ all output with a snapshot, I wanted it to be clear at a glance that the test passes thanks to specific properties (such as providerExecuted).

This makes the test more robust, because it doesn't risk breaking due to changes to the structure that have nothing to do with this fix.

## Example Scenario

**Before the fix** - `providerMetadata` is ignored during `input-streaming`:

```typescript
// Provider sends tool-input-start with providerMetadata
const stream = [
  { type: 'tool-input-start', toolCallId: 'tool-1', toolName: 'search',
    providerMetadata: { myProvider: { parentToolCallId: 'parent-123' } } },
  { type: 'tool-input-delta', toolCallId: 'tool-1', inputTextDelta: '{"query":' },
  // ...
];

// Consumer tries to access metadata during streaming
for await (const update of processedStream) {
  const toolPart = update.message.parts.find(p => p.toolCallId === 'tool-1');
  console.log(toolPart.callProviderMetadata);
  // ❌ BEFORE: undefined (metadata ignored during input-streaming)
}
```

**After the fix** - `providerMetadata` is available immediately:

```typescript
console.log(toolPart.callProviderMetadata);
// ✅ AFTER: { myProvider: { parentToolCallId: 'parent-123' } }
```

## Concrete Use Case

This fix enables [ai-sdk-provider-claude-code#100](https://github.com/ben-vargas/ai-sdk-provider-claude-code/pull/100), which adds `parentToolCallId` to track subagent hierarchy. Claude Code's Task tool spawns subagents that execute their own tools - UIs need this metadata during streaming to:
- Show which tools belong to which agent
- Build collapsible agent groups  
- Understand the execution hierarchy in real-time

Without this fix, the hierarchy metadata isn't available until `input-available`, preventing real-time hierarchy visualization during tool streaming.

