I feel like if we're going to add a `providerOptions` field and it's native to the `model`, then we should store that in the model rather than in `experimental_streamTranscribe`.

To me it makes more sense because if you changed the model, then the `providerOptions` also inherently have to change.

My proposed shape:

```typescript
const result = experimental_streamTranscribe({
  model: openai.transcription('gpt-realtime-whisper', options: {
      language: 'en',
      streaming: {delay: 'low'}
  }),
  audio,
  inputAudioFormat: { type: 'audio/pcm', rate: 24000 },
});
```

> ```ts
> const result = experimental_streamTranscribe({
>   model: openai.transcription('gpt-realtime-whisper', options: {
>       language: 'en',
>       streaming: {delay: 'low'}
>   }),
>   audio,
>   inputAudioFormat: { type: 'audio/pcm', rate: 24000 },
> });
> ```

we used to have 2nd arguments in the model methods like `openai.chat(model, options)`, but removed them for support of gateways. So the above would be

```ts
const result = experimental_streamTranscribe({
  model: openai.transcription('gpt-realtime-whisper'),
  audio,
  inputAudioFormat: { type: 'audio/pcm', rate: 24000 },
  providerOptions: {
    openai: {
      language: 'en',
      streaming: {delay: 'low'}
    }
  }
});
```

I would keep this scoped to the transport boundary, but make the contract executable.

Moving the host tool schema catalog out of the child-process environment is the right fix for the E2BIG class. The regression I would want is not only a large-catalog happy path, but also the exact failure modes around where that catalog now lives.

A useful test shape:

1. create enough host tool schemas to exceed the single environment-value limit;
2. assert the opencode process starts with only a short schema-path env value;
3. assert the local MCP bridge loads the file and exposes the same tool names and input schemas;
4. assert a missing, unreadable, or malformed schema file produces a fatal bridge error that reaches the host stream instead of an empty turn;
5. assert the legacy inline env fallback still works for small catalogs until it is intentionally removed;
6. assert the schema file is written under the run workspace and is not reused across sessions.

That keeps the file handoff from becoming a second hidden authority channel. The model-visible tool catalog, the MCP bridge catalog, and the host relay target should all be tied to the same turn/session record.

I would leave the separate headless permission-mode behavior as a follow-up, as the PR already does, but the fatal-error surfacing test belongs with this transport fix because otherwise E2BIG-style failures can still look like successful empty turns.

